# Service-specific instructions for SERVER runbooks
# Common instructions are loaded from common_instructions.toml

system = '''You are generating a runbook for SERVER issues.

SERVICE-SPECIFIC CONTEXT:
- Server issues include: CPU/memory/disk/network utilization, process issues, service failures
- Use OS-specific commands: Windows (PowerShell) or Linux (shell commands)
- Get-Counter format: Get-Counter -Counter '\Processor(_Total)\% Processor Time' (single quotes, single backslashes)
- Use {{server_name}} placeholder for server names

COMMAND FORMATTING:
- Windows: Use PowerShell commands (Get-Counter, Get-Process, Stop-Process, Restart-Service, etc.)
- Linux: Use shell commands (top, ps, free, df, kill, systemctl, etc.)

REMEDIATION COMMANDS (Windows):
- Stop-Process -Id <pid> -Force (kill process)
- Restart-Service <name> (restart service)
- Clear-EventLog -LogName <name> (clear logs)
- Remove-Item -Path <path> -Recurse -Force (delete files/folders)

REMEDIATION COMMANDS (Linux):
- kill -9 <pid> (kill process)
- systemctl restart <service> (restart service)
- rm -rf <path> (delete files/folders)
- truncate -s 0 <logfile> (clear log file)
'''

user_template = '''Generate a troubleshooting runbook YAML for this SERVER issue.

ISSUE: {issue_description}
SERVICE: {service}
ENV: {env}
RISK: {risk}
OS TYPE: {os_type}

CONTEXT (similar runbooks for reference):
{context}

THINK:
1. What metric is mentioned? (CPU/memory/disk/network)
2. What OS? (Windows/Linux)
3. What's the problem? (high utilization, full, slow, error)
4. What commands should I use? (Windows: Get-Counter/Get-Process, Linux: top/ps/free/df)

PLAN:
- Precheck 1: Check the actual metric (e.g., CPU usage for CPU issues)
- Precheck 2: Validate connectivity or system state
- Precheck 3: Additional validation
- Steps: Use iterative troubleshooting pattern (3-30 steps allowed)
  * Diagnose -> Remediate -> Diagnose -> Remediate -> Verify pattern
  * Consider branching: if first fix fails, try alternative fix
  * At least 2 steps must be remediation (40% minimum remediation ratio)
- Postcheck: Verify same metric is now normal

BRANCHING RECOMMENDATION:
For complex issues, use branching to try alternative fixes:
- Step 1: Try primary fix (on_success: 2, on_failure: 3)
- Step 2: Verify primary fix worked
- Step 3: Try alternative fix (on_success: 4, on_failure: 5)
- Step 4: Verify alternative fix worked
- Step 5: Escalate if both failed

EACH STEP MUST INCLUDE:
- name: [Step name]
  type: command
  command: [OS-specific command]
  description: [What this step does]
  expected_output: [Expected result]
  purpose: diagnose OR remediate OR verify (MANDATORY - see rules below)
  severity: safe OR moderate OR high
  skip_in_auto_mode: false
- requires_metric: which metric it uses (cpu, memory_percent, disk_free, network_latency, etc.) - optional
- captures_variable: optional variable name to store data
- depends_on: optional list of variables this step needs

PURPOSE FIELD RULES - CRITICAL:
The "purpose:" field is MANDATORY and must be set correctly:
- purpose: diagnose - For steps that ONLY gather information (Get-Process, Get-Counter, top, ps, free, df, systemctl status, journalctl)
- purpose: remediate - For steps that FIX issues (Stop-Process, Restart-Service, kill, systemctl restart, rm, truncate, clear)
- purpose: verify - For the final step that confirms the issue is resolved

EXAMPLES:
Windows:
- "Get-Process | Sort-Object CPU -Descending" -> purpose: diagnose (only lists processes)
- "Stop-Process -Id 1234 -Force" -> purpose: remediate (kills process)
- "Restart-Service w3svc" -> purpose: remediate (restarts service)
- "Clear-EventLog -LogName Application" -> purpose: remediate (clears logs)

Linux:
- "ps aux | grep process" -> purpose: diagnose (only lists processes)
- "kill -9 1234" -> purpose: remediate (kills process)
- "systemctl restart nginx" -> purpose: remediate (restarts service)
- "truncate -s 0 /var/log/app.log" -> purpose: remediate (clears log file)

MANDATORY: At least 2 of your steps MUST have "purpose: remediate". At least 40% of steps should be remediation. You can have multiple diagnostic steps for iterative troubleshooting. Diagnostic-only runbooks will be REJECTED.

Generate the YAML following the structure:
runbook_id: rb-server-[keyword]
version: 1.0.0
title: Fix [issue summary]
service: {service}
env: {env}
risk: {risk}
description: [Single-line description of issue and impact]

inputs:
- name: server_name
  type: string
  required: true
  description: Target server hostname or IP address

prechecks:
- description: [Check the actual metric from issue]
  command: [OS-specific command to check metric]
  expected_output: [What to expect]
- description: [Validate connectivity/system state]
  command: [ping or system check]
  expected_output: [Expected result]
- description: [Additional validation]
  command: [Relevant check]
  expected_output: [Expected result]

steps:
[Generate 3-30 steps following iterative pattern: diagnose -> remediate -> diagnose -> remediate -> verify. Use branching (on_success/on_failure) for alternative fixes if needed.]

postchecks:
- description: [Verify the metric from issue is now normal]
  command: [Same metric check as precheck 1]
  expected_output: [Metric is now below threshold]
'''

